n the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n\n  Line 57: 5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n\n  Line 71: Do not apply this anti-signal when the same request asks to modify executable code, parser logic, tests, shell behavior, TypeScript/Python entrypoints, JSON schema behavior, or routing algorithms. In that case, keep SKILLED surface detection and load `sk-code`.\n\n  Line 78: - Unsupported surfaces stay UNKNOWN even if intent scoring is strong.\n\n  Line 109: | CODE_QUALITY | SKILLED only: `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` plus the language-matching checklist, both when SKILLED is a resolved surface. A foreign surface loads the floor in `code-quality-standards.md` and its own declared rows |\n\n  Line 185: If no supported surface matches, ask:\n\n  Line 190: 4. Should a new `sk-code` route be planned before implementation?\n\n  Line 192: Canonical sk-code owns the SKILLED surface, the `sk-code-ci` GitHub Actions surface and the stack-agnostic `sk-code-frontend` surface for web practice. Go, Next.js server code and React Native surfaces live in a project fork.\n\n  Line 198: - Confirm the detected surface (a declared specific surface, the SKILLED fallback or UNKNOWN)\n\n  Line 199: - Confirm the task intent (implementation / debugging / verification / etc.)\n\n  Line 209: - Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n\n  Line 211: - Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n\n  Line 225: This projection is intentionally lossy in two documented ways the flat dictionary cannot express, both enforced by the prose contract and the surface-detection pseudocode in this file's §11:\n\n  Line 233: # surface-specific resource. These are loaded on every route.\n\n  Line 241:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n\n  Line 260:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"what padding should this have\", \"how much padding\", \"what font size should this be\", \"spacing between elements\", \"spacing scale\", \"type scale\", \"build a color palette\", \"shade ramp\", \"box shadow elevation\", \"border radius consistency\", \"contrast ratio failure\", \"grey text on colored background\", \"dark mode palette\", \"concentric border radius\", \"layered shadows\", \"shadow color on dark surface\", \"tabular numbers\", \"what should this button look like\", \"colour decision\", \"color decision\", \"padding\", \"type scale for\", \"font size\", \"colour palette\", \"color palette\", \"contrast ratio\", \"corner radius\", \"box shadow\", \"spacing between\", \"contrast of\", \"check the contrast\", \"colour contrast\", \"color contrast\", \"muted text\"]},\n\n  Line 263:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\":      {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"figma to code\", \"check built screen against figma\", \"compare build with figma frame\", \"figma frame\", \"figma frame check\", \"implement a figma design\"]},\n\n  Line 284:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",  # SKILLED only: the house checklist loads when SKILLED resolves, never for a foreign surface\n\n  Line 442: The router does NOT load the whole matched-intent union. After surface detection (§1, `stack-detection.md`), a route loads:\n\n  Line 446: - only the **detected surface's** slice (`<surface>/references/*`) for the matched intents.\n\n  Line 448: - It does not load another surface's resources.\n\n  Line 449: - It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n\n  Line 451: - An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n\n  Line 453: This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n\n  Line 459: - Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n\n  Line 460: - One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n\n  Line 461: - A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n\n  Line 462: - No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n\n  Line 468: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n\n  Line 481: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n\n  Line 483: > Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n\n  Line 485: **Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n\n  Line 486: Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n\n  Line 487: When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n\n  Line 489: `SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n\n  Line 490: With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n\n  Line 499: - **`workflowMode`** - the public mode/packet key: `sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review` (workflow) or `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled` (surface).\n\n  Line 500: - **`packetKind`** - the axis: `workflow` (a mode that acts) or `surface` (read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode).\n\n  Line 501: - **`backendKind`** - which backend runs the packet: `surface-router` or `review-cache` for workflow modes, `evidence-base` for surface packets.\n\n  Line 504: The advisor still routes the single identity `sk-code`, and the hub bundles zero-or-more surfaces as evidence via `routerPolicy.outcomes.surfaceBundle`.\n\n  Line 505: The workflow mode comes first, followed by surfaces.\n\n  Line 507: Bundled surfaces follow the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order. It ranks `sk-code-frontend` ahead of `sk-code-ci`, then `sk-code-skilled` last, because a new surface goes ahead of the existing ones and the SKILLED fallback stays last.\n\n  Line 511: Surface detection lives once in the hub's `shared/` layer and every mode and surface consumes it.\n\n  Line 512: It tries every declared specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n\n  Line 513: When a specific surface and `SKILLED` both match, the specific one leads and `SKILLED` comes along as second evidence.\n\n  Line 514: Each surface declares its markers in a `surfaceDetection` block in `mode-registry.json`, or in its own `graph-metadata.json` when it lives outside the hub.\n\n  Line 515: Modes own workflow contracts, not surface identity.\n\n  Line 516: `sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are the surfaces that stack detection does not select. The hub bundles both from request vocabulary alone: CI through `code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and Frontend through `code-frontend-aliases` in `hub-router.json`.\n\n  Line 520: When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n\n  Line 531:     \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n\n  Line 532:     \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n\n  Line 573:     for surface_mode in surface_bundle_from_hub_router(task):\n\n\n(Results are truncated: showing first 60 results. Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md\",\"pattern\":\"defer|compiled-route|surface|implement\",\"limit\":60}"},"time":{"start":1794561687334,"end":1794561687381}}}}
